\uc1
\pard\fi0\li0\qj\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f3\lang1033\fs24 history:
\par \pard\li720\fi-360\jclisttab\tx0\qj\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 {\listtext\pard\plain\f6\fs24\lang1024 \u-4051 ?\tab}\ls3\ilvl0 scribe: {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezper[0].4100/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f4\fs24\chcbpat17 Abraham ben R. Samuel Picar\u7789 ?iya}}\plain \f3\lang1033\fs24  (?) (\plain \f2\lang1037\fs24 \u1508 \'f4\u1497 \'e9\u1511 \'f7\u1512 \'f8\u1496 \'e8\u1497 \'e9\u1497 \'e9\u1488 \'e0\plain \f3\lang1033\fs24 ), born in \ldblquote Weisnae\rdblquote  [=Weisenau?] close to the river Rhine, close to Mainz (\ldblquote natus in pago Weisnae, Rheno urbique Monguntiae vicino\rdblquote ) (cf. colophon on fol. 158a and 166b)
\plain\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f6\fs24\lang1024 \u-4051 ?\tab}\ls3\ilvl0 \plain \f3\lang1033\fs24 completion of the codex: Rukingen close to the river Kaunitz (\plain \f2\lang1037\fs24 \u1511 \'f7\u1493 \'e5\u1497 \'e9\u1504 \'f0\u1497 \'e9\u1509 \'f5\plain \f3\lang1033\fs24 ), close to Frankfurt am Main, 1577 (exact date: night of the 6\plain \f3\lang1033\fs16 th\plain \f3\lang1033\fs24  day of the week, 14\plain \f3\lang1033\fs16 th\plain \f3\lang1033\fs24  of Adar, Purim [Latin: \ldblquote Dies Sortium\rdblquote ] (\ldblquote in festo Sortium minori\rdblquote )
\plain\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f6\fs24\lang1024 \u-4051 ?\tab}\ls3\ilvl0 \plain \f3\lang1033\fs24 The codex (B. H. 4. 33) represents the \u8222 \'84\plain \f2\lang1037\fs24 \u1504 \'f0\u1510 \'f6\u1495 \'e7\u1493 \'e5\u1503 \'ef\plain \f3\lang1033\fs24 \ldblquote , which was originally composed in 1399 by {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezper[1].4100/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f3\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat17 Yom Tov Lipman}}\plain \f3\lang1033\fs24  and an augmented (? \u8222 \'84auctoque\ldblquote ) version, yet approved (\ldblquote recognito\rdblquote ) by the author himself. (\u8222 \'84Hoc Ni\u231 \'e7ahon ex recognito auctoque ab ipso auctore exemplari expressum repraesentat codex noster [\u8230 \'85].\ldblquote  )
\plain\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f6\fs24\lang1024 \u-4051 ?\tab}\ls3\ilvl0 {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezper[2].4100/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f3\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat17 Wagenseil}}\plain \f3\lang1033\fs24  used the codex for the emendation and supplementation of Theodoricus Hackspan's [theologian, hebraist] edition
\plain\par \pard\fi-360\li720\qj\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f3\lang1033\fs24 
\par \pard\fi0\li0\qj\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 David, Die hebr\u228 \'e4ische Handschriftensammlung, S. 312: \u8222 \'84\plain \f3\i\lang1033\fs24 Sefer Nitzachon\plain \f3\lang1033\fs24  (Buch des Sieges) wurde von R. Jom Tov Lipmann M\u252 \'fchlhausen verfasst, einem Rabbiner, der vom 14. zum 15. Jahrhundert in Prag lebte. In Leipzig sind zwei Abschriften vorhanden. Die eine wurde 1619 angefertigt ({\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://5007[0].501m/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f3\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat17 B.H. 4. 27}}\plain \f3\lang1033\fs24 ), die andere (B.H. 4.33) im Jahre 1577. Diesen Text lie\u223 \'df Johann Christian Wagenseil 1644 in Altdorf drucken.\ldblquote }

