\pard\fi0\li0\qj\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs24 content:
\par \pard\li720\fi-360\jclisttab\tx0\qj\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\fs24\lang1024 \u-4051 ?\tab}\ls1\ilvl0 consists of two parts with 46 {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezwrk[0].6922/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat17 songs}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24 , for the most part for the worship on Sabbath, some marriage (\u8222 \'84nuptialia\ldblquote ) and some love (\u8222 \'84amatoria\ldblquote ) songs and one of them (the 30\plain \f1\lang1033\fs16 th\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24 ) jokingly ridicules the customs of the Jews living in the Rhine area; Delitzsch marks 26 of them as very rare and probably not yet edited; many were never used in the ritual of Jews from Germany (?) (\u8222 \'84in ritum nostratium Judaeorum\ldblquote ), but were customary in Oriental congregations/ synagogues (as can be shown from the names of those by whom the Jews (\u8222 \'84Israel\ldblquote ) are said to be oppressed and the \u8222 \'84Asian\ldblquote  way of speaking (\u8222 \'84genus dicendi asiaticum\ldblquote ); Delitzsch praises the quality of the songs as outstanding; the names of some of the authors were later added by the scribe:
\plain\par \pard\li1080\fi-360\jclisttab\tx0\qj\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\fs24\lang1024 \u-4051 ?\tab}\ls1\ilvl1 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs24 1.  \endash  ends with a (rhyming) colophon, in which the name of the scribe ({\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "hida://bezper[0].4100/?u"}{\fldrslt \plain \f2\lang1037\fs24\chcbpat17 \u8222 \'84Jacob\ldblquote / \u1497 \'e9\u1506 \'f2\u1511 \'f7\u1489 \'e1}}\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24 ) is mentioned
\plain\par \pard\fi0\li0\qj\ri0\sb0\sa0\itap0 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24 the two short texts on fol. 31v are bleached out}
